const verdicts=["One tray, serious flavour and no mountain of washing-up. Even you can survive that.","Crunchy outside, juicy inside and miles better than the beige freezer rubbish.","Fast, filthy and ready before a delivery driver has found your street.","A loaded bowl with actual flavour. Gym food can stop being a punishment now.","Rich, creamy and completely uninterested in your diet plan.","Messy hands, full stomach, zero regret. Keep the napkins close.","Cheap ingredients bullied into tasting far more expensive than they are.","Sticky, glossy and loud enough to make plain boiled vegetables feel embarrassed.","Both hands required. Dignity optional. That is how a burger should behave.","Deep, comforting flavour from one pot. Try not to take credit for all the hard work."];
function generatedSteps(food:string,base:string,seasoning:string,style:number,variant:number){
 const names=["chicken","turkey","beef","pork","salmon","prawns","halloumi","chickpeas","lentils","mushrooms","sausages","beans"];
 const label=names[variant];
 const finish=["Taste it before serving. Guessing is how bland dinners happen.","Get it onto hot plates. You did not come this far to serve lukewarm disappointment.","Give it one final look, then stop fiddling and feed people.","Season it properly at the end. Salt is cheaper than pretending it is fine."][variant%4];
 const airTime=["14–16","12–14","8–10","12–14","8–10","7–9","10–12","12–14","10–12","9–11","12–14","10–12"][variant];
 const sets=[
  [`Heat the oven to 210°C before you start chopping. A cold oven turns dinner into a long, damp apology.`,`Cut the ${base}, onion and ${food} into even pieces. If half are boulders and half are crumbs, do not act surprised when they cook differently.`,`Mix the ${seasoning} with the oil, salt and pepper, then rub it over everything until every piece is properly coated.`,`Spread it across a large tray in one layer. Crowd it and you will steam it; this is a traybake, not a sauna.`,`Roast for 20 minutes, then turn the ${base} and move the darker pieces away from the edges. Use your eyes, not blind faith in a timer.`,`Return it for another 15–20 minutes until the ${label} is cooked and the edges are properly caramelised. Pale food tastes of surrender.`,`Rest it for 3 minutes so the juices stay in the food instead of flooding the tray.`,finish],
  [`Heat the air fryer to 200°C before touching the ${food}. Standing around waiting later is not part of the recipe.`,`Pat the ${food} dry, then cut it into evenly sized bites. Wet food does not crisp; it sulks.`,`Mix the ${seasoning}, salt and pepper with a spoonful of oil, then coat every piece properly.`,`Arrange the ${label} in one layer with space around it. If you pile it up, you have built a tiny steam room.`,`Cook for ${airTime} minutes, shaking or turning halfway so both sides get a fair chance at becoming delicious.`,`Check the centre is cooked before celebrating. Colour on the outside is not a legal defence for raw food.`,`Crisp the ${base} separately, then toss everything together while it is still hot enough to hold the seasoning.`,finish],
  [`Put a pan of salted water on a hard boil and get the ${base} in. Noodles wait for nobody and turn to wallpaper paste out of spite.`,`Mix the ${seasoning} with two tablespoons of water, a pinch of pepper and the garlic before the wok gets hot.`,`Slice the ${food} evenly so it cooks at the same speed. This is dinner, not a collection of random offcuts.`,`Heat the wok until properly hot, add the oil and sear the ${label} without poking it every six seconds.`,`Add the onion and vegetables and stir-fry for 2–3 minutes so they stay crisp instead of collapsing into sadness.`,`Drain the noodles one minute early, tip them into the wok and pour over the sauce.`,`Toss hard for 60–90 seconds until glossy and clinging. Sauce belongs on the noodles, not abandoned at the bottom.`,finish],
  [`Rinse the ${base} until the water runs clear. Skip this and you are making glue with toppings.`,`Cook it with a good pinch of salt, then leave it covered for 10 minutes. Stop lifting the lid like something exciting is happening.`,`Season the ${food} with ${seasoning}, salt and pepper while the rice behaves itself.`,`Sear the ${label} in a hot pan until the edges are deeply coloured. Grey is not a flavour and never will be.`,`Add the onion and garlic, cook for 3 minutes, then splash in enough water to lift every sticky bit from the pan.`,`Fluff the rice with a fork. A spoon crushes it, and it has suffered enough.`,`Build the bowls with rice first, then the ${label}, vegetables and every drop of pan juice.`,finish],
  [`Bring a large pan of properly salted water to the boil. A shy pinch of salt in four litres of water achieves absolutely nothing.`,`Cook the ${base} for two minutes less than the packet says and save a mug of pasta water before draining. Future you will be grateful for once.`,`Brown the ${food} in a wide pan, working in batches if necessary. Overcrowding gives you grey, sweaty disappointment.`,`Add the onion and cook until soft, then stir in the garlic and ${seasoning} for 30 seconds. Do not burn it now and blame the recipe.`,`Lower the heat before adding the creamy sauce. Boiling dairy until it splits is an impressive waste of ingredients.`,`Add the pasta and enough reserved water to make the sauce cling silkily instead of sitting in a sad puddle.`,`Toss for one minute, finish with black pepper and keep a close eye on the pan because creamy pasta thickens fast.`,finish],
  [`Warm a heavy frying pan until you can feel the heat above it. Lukewarm pans make pale, floppy fillings.`,`Coat the ${food} with ${seasoning}, salt and a little oil, then leave it for 5 minutes while you slice the onion.`,`Sear the ${label} in batches until browned and cooked through. If the pan goes watery, you ignored the previous sentence.`,`Char the onion in the same pan so it picks up all the seasoned bits. Those bits are flavour; do not wash them away.`,`Warm the ${base} for 20 seconds per side so it bends instead of cracking like old paint.`,`Slice or spoon the filling into the centre, then add the charred onion and something sharp or crunchy.`,`Fold the sides in before rolling tightly. A loose taco has one goal: decorating your shirt.`,finish],
  [`Get the rice cooking first. Curry finished beside a pan of raw rice is the sort of planning disaster we are avoiding.`,`Heat the oil and fry the onion for 6–8 minutes until genuinely golden, not merely warm and translucent.`,`Add the garlic and ${seasoning}, then fry for a full minute. Raw curry powder tastes like the back of a dusty cupboard.`,`Stir in the ${food} and turn it through the spices until every piece is coated and starting to colour.`,`Add the ${base} and enough water to simmer, scraping up anything stuck to the bottom before it burns into bitterness.`,`Bubble gently until the ${label} is tender and the sauce is thick enough to coat a spoon. Violent boiling does not make it finish faster.`,`Taste it. Add salt, then a squeeze of something sharp if the sauce feels heavy. Your tongue is part of the equipment.`,finish],
  [`Mix the ${seasoning} with two tablespoons of water before the pan gets hot. Scrambling for bottles mid-cook is how dinner becomes a crime scene.`,`Cut the ${food} into even pieces and pat it dry. Moisture is the sworn enemy of a decent sear.`,`Heat the pan until smoking, add the oil and cook the ${label} quickly until browned, then remove it.`,`Throw in the onion and vegetables and leave them alone long enough to blister. Constant stirring is nervousness, not technique.`,`Return the ${label}, pour in the sauce and toss over high heat until everything is lacquered and sticky.`,`Add the ${base} and keep tossing for another minute so it absorbs the sauce instead of merely sitting underneath it.`,`Take it off the heat while the vegetables still have some bite. Soggy broccoli is an avoidable tragedy.`,finish],
  [`Get the pan or air fryer properly hot before building the burger. Starting cold is how you make a greasy hockey puck.`,`Season the ${food} with salt, pepper and ${seasoning}, then shape it gently. Knead it like bread and it will eat like a tyre.`,`Make the patties slightly wider than the ${base}; they shrink as they cook because physics has no respect for your plans.`,`Cook until a dark crust forms, then flip once. Stop pressing them down and squeezing all the good stuff into the pan.`,`Add cheese for the final 2 minutes and cover or close the drawer so it melts rather than merely sweating on top.`,`Toast the ${base} cut-side down until golden. A cold, soft bun ruins a burger faster than cheap sauce.`,`Stack with pickles and sauce, keeping the wet ingredients away from the bottom bun unless you enjoy structural collapse.`,finish],
  [`Put a heavy casserole over high heat and brown the ${food} in batches. One pot does not mean one shade of grey.`,`Move the browned ${label} aside, lower the heat and soften the onion until sweet and golden.`,`Add the garlic and ${seasoning} for one minute, stirring so it blooms without welding itself to the pot.`,`Tip in the ${base}, then scrape the bottom hard enough to release every dark sticky bit. That is free flavour; take it.`,`Return the ${label}, add enough liquid to almost cover and bring it to a gentle simmer.`,`Cover and cook slowly until tender, stirring occasionally. If it is boiling like a volcano, turn the bloody heat down.`,`Remove the lid for the final 10 minutes if the sauce looks thin, then let it rest for 5 minutes before serving.`,finish]
 ]; return sets[style];
}
